1. Introduction
Parkinson's disease (PD) is the second most prevalent neurodegenerative disorder and the fastest growing in terms of disability burden, in a context where neurological disorders collectively affect 3.40 billion people worldwide [1]. Clinical diagnosis of PD remains imperfect, with reported accuracy between 73% and 84% [2], and symptom overlap with other movement disorders complicates early-stage identification. Although PD is primarily characterized by motor symptoms (tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, postural instability), it is increasingly recognized as a multisystem disorder involving cognitive, autonomic, and sleep-related manifestations [3,4]. This heterogeneity motivates multimodal data-driven approaches that integrate complementary signals to support earlier and more reliable detection [5].
From a clinical-workflow perspective, the practical promise of multimodal AI for PD is a screening or triage tool that combines low-burden wearable and questionnaire data to flag patients who warrant specialist neurological assessment. Realizing that promise safely requires that reported performance gains be genuine rather than artifacts of how a study was evaluated: a decision-support tool validated under a leaky protocol risks overstating its benefit once deployed on truly unseen patients, with consequences for both clinical trust and patient safety. This concern motivates the present study.
Multimodal machine learning addresses the detection need by combining sensor data, clinical scales, neuroimaging, voice, and patient reported outcomes, consistently outperforming unimodal baselines for PD detection [6-8]. However, three obstacles limit clinical translation. First, patient cohort mismatches and temporal misalignment
yield incomplete multimodal profiles. Second, fusion mechanisms struggle to preserve crossmodal semantics when combining highdimensional sources (e.g., sensor time-series) with low-dimensional ones (e.g., questionnaires). Third, severe class imbalance at modality intersections biases learning toward majority classes [9]. Generative augmentation, particularly through Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), has been proposed to mitigate scarcity and imbalance [10,11].
A common pipeline in multimodal clinical learning combines two operations: embedding (via autoencoders) to reduce heterogeneity, and balancing (via SMOTE or GANs) to address class imbalance. While latent-space augmentation has been explored in unimodal settings, such as imbalanced image classification [12] and ECG beat classification [13], the relative ordering of embedding and balancing operations in heterogeneous multimodal clinical pipelines has received little systematic attention, and the methodological risk of split-after-augmentation leakage has received even less. Practitioners typically apply augmentation either before embedding (in raw input space) or after embedding (in latent-space). Yet these two orderings make fundamentally different assumptions about the geometry of the data: rawspace generation must reproduce the highdimensional manifold structure directly, while latent-space generation operates on a compact, disentangled representation.
In this work, we systematically compare these two pipeline orderings on heterogeneous multimodal clinical data, with an explicit focus on whether reported gains survive a leakage-free evaluation. We define Embed-Then-Balance (ETB), which projects modalities into a unified latent-space before applying GAN-based augmentation, and Balance-Then-Embed (BTE), which generates synthetic samples in the raw


3
input space before encoding. The principal contributions of this article are:
- A general, modality-agnostic framework for the ordering of embedding and balancing operations, with a manifold-hypothesis argument for why this order is expected to affect the geometry of the synthetic samples each pipeline produces.
- A leakage-free evaluation on a heterogeneous multimodal clinical dataset. We instantiate both orderings on the PADS Parkinson's disease dataset, which combines wearable sensor time-series, clinical scales, and a nonmotor symptom questionnaire, and we evaluate them under a cross-validation protocol that fits every preprocessing and augmentation step on training data only. We further introduce a quantitative decomposition framework that separates the apparent gain into a baseline term, a residual augmentation effect, and a split-afteraugmentation leakage term — a methodological audit tool directly applicable to other clinical AI evaluations.
- A generative quality evaluation of both orderings, assessed with a classifier twosample test, the Wasserstein distance, and the distance-to-closest-record, showing that the two orderings differ markedly in the fidelity of the data they generate even where they do not differ in classification.
- An explicit discussion of the clinical implications of these findings for the design and audit of augmentation-based decisionsupport pipelines (Section 6.5).
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 reviews related work. Section 3 presents the proposed framework. Section 4 describes the experimental setup. Section 5 reports the results. Section 6 discusses limitations, clinical implications, and interpretation. Section 7 concludes.
2. Related Work
To position our contribution, we organize prior work into four areas: multimodal learning for
neurodegenerative disease diagnosis, generative augmentation under class imbalance, fusion strategies in clinical pipelines, and prior work on the PADS dataset. Table S1 (Supplementary Material) compares our approach to representative methods across these areas.
2.1 Multimodal Learning for Neurodegenerative Disease Diagnosis
Unimodal approaches have demonstrated effectiveness in extracting disease-specific patterns from individual data sources, including EEG signals for Alzheimer's disease [14] and Parkinson's disease [15], spiral drawings [16], and structural MRI [17]. While these methods achieve high accuracy on their target modality, they cannot exploit the complementary information distributed across the motor, cognitive, and autonomic channels affected by PD.
Multimodal approaches address this limitation by integrating heterogeneous signals. Sensor-based monitoring captures gait and tremor in naturalistic settings [18], while combinations of imaging, handwriting, clinical scales, and patientreported outcomes consistently outperform unimodal baselines for early PD detection [6,19,20]. The same principle has been validated for Alzheimer's disease, where deep learning models combining structural MRI, genetic markers, and cognitive tests reach diagnostic performance comparable to expert neurologists [21].
2.2 Generative Augmentation under Class Imbalance
Class imbalance and data scarcity are pervasive in clinical ML, and Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) [22] have emerged as a primary tool for synthetic data generation in healthcare [23]. For multivariate clinical timeseries, recurrent and conditional GANs have shown that synthetic samples can preserve


4
predictive utility while addressing class imbalance [24]. Extensions of this paradigm have been applied to PD-specific tasks: GAN-based augmentation of spiral-drawing images improves classification accuracy when training data are limited [25].
A central methodological question concerns where in the pipeline augmentation should occur. Two strategies dominate practice. The first generates synthetic samples directly in the raw input space, then encodes them downstream; this preserves all original feature dimensions but requires the generator to model the full highdimensional manifold, which is challenging for heterogeneous clinical data. The second strategy first projects inputs into a compact latent-space via autoencoders, then performs augmentation within this representation. Latent-space augmentation has been explored in unimodal settings: [12] couple a variational autoencoder with a GAN (IDA-GAN) to generate minorityclass samples in latent-space for imbalanced image classification, and [13] apply a similar autoencoder-plus-GAN strategy to ECG beat classification, reporting AUC improvements of 2% in both binary and multiclass settings. These works establish the value of latent-space augmentation but remain confined to singlemodality data and, notably, do not audit their reported gains for split-after-augmentation leakage. Privacy considerations have also shaped this literature: [26] formalize the trade-off between predictive utility and membershipinference risk for clinical time-series GANs, motivating the inclusion of distance-based privacy metrics in synthetic data evaluation.
2.3 Multimodal Fusion Strategies
Multimodal representation learning seeks to project heterogeneous inputs into a shared latentspace where semantically similar content is encoded by similar vectors [27]. Formally, em = f(x1, ..., xn), where xi ∈ R^di denote modality
specific representations and em ∈ R^dshared is the fused embedding. Three fusion regimes are commonly distinguished. Early fusion concatenates modality-specific embeddings before downstream processing, producing finegrained joint representations but requiring careful temporal and dimensional alignment; this strategy has been used for PD classification combining handwriting, drawing, and clinical scores [6]. Intermediate fusion introduces attention or cross-modal gating mechanisms that weight modality contributions during processing, capturing non-linear inter-modal dependencies; recent PD work in this category includes crossmodal attention with contrastive learning between MRI and clinical scales [28], semisupervised speech embedding fusion [29], and smartphone-based fusion of voice, finger tapping, and gait [30]. Late fusion integrates independent modality-specific decisions and is robust to missing modalities, but cannot exploit featurelevel cross-modal correlations.
Each fusion regime presents trade-offs between expressiveness, robustness, and computational cost. We adopt early fusion because it provides the cleanest experimental setting for isolating the effect of augmentation ordering, our primary research question. The choice of fusion strategy is orthogonal to the ETB-versus-BTE comparison, which can in principle be applied to any of the three regimes.
2.4 Prior Work on the PADS Dataset
The PADS dataset [31] was introduced together with an initial ML benchmark by [32], who reported balanced accuracies of 91.16% for PDversus-HC and 72.42% for PD-versus-DD using an integrative pipeline combining classical signal processing and deep learning on the smartwatch sensor stream. More recent work has extended this benchmark: [33] propose MFAM, a multiscale frequency-aware adversarial multi-instance network that leverages medical priors for PD


5
versus-DD severity assessment on PADS. These prior efforts focus on model architecture for signal-based classification, but do not address the orthogonal question of how augmentation strategies should be combined with multimodal embedding when class imbalance is severe, nor whether their reported gains are leakage-free.
Table S1 (Supplementary Material) summarizes the position of our contribution against representative prior work across four criteria: (i) domain and data scope (healthcare, multimodal); (ii) methodological pillars (fusion, embedding, augmentation); (iii) modeling strategy (patient correspondence, multiclass capability); and (iv) evaluation rigor (classification metrics, generative quality metrics, leakage control). Prior work typically addresses a subset of these criteria. Unimodal approaches lack fusion robustness; augmentation-focused studies rarely evaluate multiclass detection performance jointly with generative quality or leakage control; fusionfocused architectures often assume complete modality availability and do not consider augmentation. To our knowledge, no prior work simultaneously (a) operates on heterogeneous multimodal clinical data, (b) systematically compares the ordering of embedding and balancing operations, (c) combines a syntheticdata fidelity protocol with downstream classification metrics, and (d) quantifies the leakage component of reported gains.
3. Proposed Framework
3.1 Overview
Multimodal clinical pipelines that must also correct class imbalance combine two operations: embedding, which projects heterogeneous modalities into a shared representation, and balancing, which adds synthetic minority samples to compensate for an imbalance ratio ρ = Nmajority/Nminority ≫ 1. These two operations can be applied in either order, a choice usually
left implicit. We make this ordering the object of study and define two pipelines that differ only in it (Table 1).
In Embed-Then-Balance (ETB), each modality is first encoded into a compact latent-space, the latent codes are fused into a unified patient representation, and synthetic minority samples are generated within that latent-space. In Balance-Then-Embed (BTE), synthetic samples are generated first, in the raw input space of each modality, and the balanced data is only then encoded and fused. The two pipelines use the same encoders, the same fusion operator, and the same classifiers; they differ solely in whether balancing precedes or follows embedding.
The framework is modality-agnostic. Each modality is routed to its own encoder and, in BTE, its own generator; modalities can be added or removed without altering the structure of the pipeline, the only requirement being to retrain the encoder (ETB) or generator (BTE) of the affected modality. An instantiation on three clinical modalities is described in Section 4.
The order is expected to matter for a geometric reason. Under the manifold hypothesis, highdimensional clinical data of dimension D concentrates near a lower-dimensional structure M ⊂ R^D. A generator acting in the raw-space must reproduce this manifold directly; samples that fall away from M are artifacts that do not correspond to plausible patients. A generator acting in a learned latent-space instead draws samples from a compact representation in which the encoder has already simplified the manifold. ETB and BTE therefore make different geometric assumptions, and raw-space generation (BTE) is expected to be more exposed to off-manifold artifacts than latent-space generation (ETB). Our experiments test this prediction directly.


