ones; training follows the minimax objective min_G max_D V(G,D) = E[log D(x)] + E[log(1 D(G(z)))]. Because the modalities have different distributions, one generator-discriminator pair is used per modality rather than a single shared one.
Many GAN variants have been proposed, including conditional GANs [34], which produce samples of a specified target class, and deep convolutional GANs [35], designed for spatial fidelity in image generation. Our choice of generator is governed by the experimental goal: because we compare operation order, the generator architecture is held fixed and deliberately kept simple, so that any difference observed between ETB and BTE can be attributed to operation order rather than to architectural sophistication. In preliminary experiments, convolutional generators yielded only marginal sample-quality gains over feedforward (MLP) generators on heterogeneous tabular inputs, while being more prone to mode collapse and unstable training. We therefore adopt a vanilla GAN [36] with dense layers, Leaky ReLU activations, and batch normalization, used identically in both pipelines. More expressive generators, such as recurrent or convolutional architectures or recent variants like WGAN-GP, CTGAN, and TimeGAN, could improve raw-space time-series generation in particular; we do not adopt them here because they would confound generator quality with the operation-order effect under study, and we leave their evaluation to future work.
4. Experimental Setup
We instantiate the framework of Section 3 on the PADS dataset, a multimodal Parkinson's disease cohort with three modalities: wearable sensor movement time-series, structured clinical scales, and a non-motor symptom questionnaire.
4.1 Dataset Description
We use the PD Smartwatch Dataset (PADS) v1.0.0 [31] to evaluate the framework. PADS combines, in a single multimodal release, highfrequency sensor data capturing motor fluctuations, structured clinical profiles, and patient-reported outcomes. The dataset contains 5,159 measurement steps from 469 participants and is divided into three diagnostic categories: Parkinson's Disease (PD), Healthy Control (HC), and Differential Diagnoses (DD), the last of which includes patients with essential tremor, atypical Parkinsonism, and multiple sclerosis. The dataset exhibits substantial class imbalance, motivating the balancing strategies introduced in our framework (Table 2).
