digital questionnaire containing 30 binary (yes/no) indicators of non-motor symptoms; missing entries were imputed during preprocessing.
● Clinical/demographic data: age, gender, height, weight, handedness, smoking history, alcohol impact on tremor, and family history of PD.
The raw dataset is organized into separate JSON files, merged by unique subject ID to construct aligned multimodal samples. Following the dataset authors' recommendations, the first 0.5 seconds of each time-series were discarded to eliminate noise artifacts caused by the smartwatch's start-recording vibration, and sensor data was z-score normalized before training.
4.2 Dataset Exploration
While the movement data demonstrates structural balance across sensor locations and tasks, the clinical and demographic attributes exhibit distributional characteristics inherent to observational cohorts (Supplementary Material, Figure S2). We observe a demographic skew with a higher prevalence of male subjects and an age distribution concentrated in the 65-75 range for the PD group, reflecting the epidemiological nature of the disease. Variables with high rates of missing values (e.g., effect of alcohol on tremor) were excluded to avoid extensive imputation artifacts. The PDNMS questionnaire is characterized by a strong majority of negative responses (73.1%), an intrinsic feature-level imbalance that complicates detection of the minority (positive symptom) class.
