4.3.2 Data Fusion and Balancing Strategy
Feature integration is performed via the early fusion strategy defined in Section 3.2, yielding a unified multimodal patient vector zfinal. To address the severe class imbalance identified in the dataset (ρ ≫ 1), we compare two categories of balancing strategies:
- SMOTE (interpolation-based): the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique generates synthetic samples via linear interpolation between existing minority-class neighbors; it serves as a standard baseline.
- GAN-based augmentation (distributionbased): a Generative Adversarial Network trained in an upsample-only mode to approximate the underlying minority-class distribution.
In ETB, the GAN is trained on the compact 90-D latent vectors, reducing the dimensionality of the


9
generative task from over 12,000 dimensions to 90 and stabilizing training. In BTE, augmentation is performed in the raw data space, requiring 13 independent GANs (11 for movement tasks, 1 for clinical, 1 for the questionnaire) before the encoding step. To maintain methodological consistency across pipelines, all GANs use a unified dense architecture (MLP) with 128 hidden units and a latent dimension (z) of 32.
4.4 Evaluation Metrics
4.4.1 Classification Metrics
Given the substantial class imbalance (ρ ≈ 4.15), standard accuracy is not a reliable performance indicator. We therefore report AUC-ROC as the primary metric for discriminative performance, since it is independent of decision thresholds. We also report F1-score to balance precision and recall, and sensitivity (recall) to quantify the model's ability to detect PD cases and minimize false negatives — both critical for clinical screening, where a missed case delays specialist referral.
4.4.2 Generative Quality Metrics
Evaluating synthetic medical data requires assessing both statistical fidelity and privacy preservation.
Classifier Two-Sample Test (C2ST): we train a discriminative classifier to separate real from synthetic samples and report the area under its ROC curve (C2ST AUC) and its accuracy. A value close to 0.5 indicates that real and synthetic samples are statistically indistinguishable (high realism); a value close to 1.0 indicates trivial separability.
Wasserstein distance: the 1st Wasserstein distance W(Pr, Pg) quantifies the minimal transport cost to transform the synthetic distribution Pg into the real one Pr, over the set of joint distributions with the correct marginals.
Privacy distance (DCR): the Distance to Closest Record measures, for each synthetic sample s, the Euclidean distance to the nearest real training record r ∈ Rreal. A DCR value approaching 0 indicates that the generator may have reproduced a record close to a real patient, a potential privacy risk relevant to any clinical deployment that shares or publishes synthetic cohorts.
4.4.3 Calibration and Clinical Utility Metrics
AUC-ROC and F1 quantify discrimination, i.e. whether the model ranks PD patients above nonPD patients, but discrimination alone does not establish whether a model is safe to use for clinical decision-making: a well-discriminating model can still be poorly calibrated, or can provide no net benefit once the cost of false positives and false negatives is taken into account. We therefore complement Section 4.4.1 with two additional analyses, computed on the same 25 fold-seed predictions per configuration, restricted to the strongest classifiers per pipeline (Random Forest, NoAug and best augmentation condition, binary task).
Calibration. We assess whether predicted probabilities reflect true outcome frequencies using (i) the Brier score, the mean squared difference between predicted probability and the binary outcome, and (ii) reliability (calibration) curves obtained by binning predicted probabilities into deciles and plotting observed PD prevalence against mean predicted probability per bin, together with the expected calibration error (ECE). Out-of-fold predictions are pooled across the 25 fold-seed repetitions before binning, and models are compared with and without Platt scaling / isotonic recalibration fitted on the training folds only, to test whether any miscalibration is correctable without retraining the classifier.
Decision curve analysis (net benefit). Discrimination and calibration do not indicate whether using the model changes clinical


10
decisions for the better. We compute the standardized net benefit of Vickers and Elkin [38] across a plausible range of threshold probabilities pt (risk thresholds at which a clinician would refer a patient for specialist neurological assessment), NB(pt) = (TP/n) - (FP/n) x (pt/(1pt)), and plot it against the two default strategies of referring all patients or referring none. A model is only clinically useful over a given threshold range if its net-benefit curve lies above both default strategies over that range. We report the range of pt over which each pipeline/classifier configuration dominates both defaults, and we repeat the analysis for the augmented and unaugmented conditions to test whether augmentation changes the clinically useful threshold range, not only the AUC.
Together, these two analyses translate the discrimination results of Section 5.1 into the two questions a clinical evaluator asks next: can the predicted risk be trusted as a probability, and does using the model, at a plausible referral threshold, do more good than harm compared with current practice.
4.4.4 Feature Importance
To ground the model in variables a clinician can interpret, we compute permutation feature importance [39] on the fused 90-D representation for the best-performing configuration (ETB/BTE, Random Forest, NoAug), averaged over the 25 fold-seed repetitions, and we group the resulting importances by modality (movement, clinical, questionnaire) and, within the clinical and questionnaire modalities, by individual variable. Because permutation importance on the fused latent-space attributes importance to embedding dimensions rather than directly to raw clinical variables, we additionally report modality-level importance obtained by zeroing out, one modality at a time, its full block of latent dimensions and measuring the resulting AUC drop, which is directly interpretable as "how much does removing this modality cost".
4.5 Evaluation Protocol
Both pipelines are evaluated with stratified 5-fold cross-validation repeated across five random seeds (42, 123, 456, 789, 2024), giving 25 independent measurements per configuration. Within each fold, the entire processing chain (feature scaling, class rebalancing, and classifier training) is fitted on the training partition only and then applied to the untouched test partition; the rebalancing step is therefore never exposed to test samples. This guards against two forms of leakage common in augmentation studies. The first, split-after-augmentation leakage, occurs when SMOTE or a GAN is applied to the full dataset before the train/test split, so that synthetic samples derived from test points enter the training set. The second occurs when the generator itself is trained on the full dataset before splitting, so that its synthetic samples encode test-set information. Both are eliminated by fitting the resampler inside each fold: for BTE, the per-modality generators and autoencoders are retrained within every fold on the training partition; for ETB, the latent-space generator is likewise retrained within every fold. We consider this protocol, rather than the classifier architecture, the primary methodological contribution relevant to clinical practice: it is directly transferable to any pipeline in which a decision-support system is retrained periodically on institutional data.
5. Results
We compare six classifiers (SVM-RBF, Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, MLP, and Logistic Regression) across the two pipelines (ETB and BTE), the three balancing conditions (no augmentation, SMOTE, GAN), and two tasks (binary and multiclass classification), all under the leakage-free protocol of Section 4.5.
