5.1.3 Comparison of ETB and BTE
Across both tasks, the two pipelines achieve equivalent classification performance: all reported configurations lie within one standard deviation of one another. Two observations characterize the practical trade-offs between them, both directly relevant to deployment choices in a clinical setting with limited computing infrastructure:
- Classifier robustness to low-fidelity synthetics. Ensemble tree models (Random
Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost) absorb the noise injected by BTE-GAN with no measurable degradation, whereas marginbased classifiers (SVM, Logistic Regression, MLP) show small negative effects (-0.04 to 0.07 AUC in multiclass). The effect of augmentation therefore depends on the classifier family, a practical consideration when selecting a model for a resourceconstrained clinical site.
- Computational cost. BTE requires training 13 independent generators (11 movement, 1 clinical, 1 PDNMS) and 13 autoencoders per cross-validation fold, against a single GAN in the unified latent-space for ETB. In our experiments, ETB completed in approximately 30 minutes versus approximately 5 hours of GPU time for BTE — a difference that matters for institutions retraining models on a recurring schedule.
The two pipelines differ unambiguously, however, in the statistical fidelity of the synthetic samples they generate, which we report next.
