5.2 Generative Quality
Synthetic-data quality is assessed with the metrics defined in Section 4.4 and summarized in Table 7.
ETB augmentation
The latent-space GAN produces statistically realistic synthetic embeddings: its C2ST AUC of 0.458 is close to the ideal value of 0.5, indicating that a discriminator struggles to separate real from synthetic embeddings. SMOTE in the same latent-space yields a higher C2ST AUC of 0.768: its interpolated points are systematically detectable as off-manifold interpolations between real neighbors. Figure 1 shows the corresponding overlap between the real and synthetic distributions for ETB-GAN.
BTE augmentation
BTE-GAN exhibits poor generative quality, with a C2ST AUC of 1.000 and a discriminator accuracy of 1.000: samples generated in the raw data space are trivially distinguishable from real ones. Its high Wasserstein distance (0.545) further indicates that BTE-GAN does not reproduce the temporal dependencies and sensor correlations of the raw signal, consistent with mode collapse and off-manifold generation in the high-dimensional raw-space.
BTE-SMOTE under flattening
SMOTE requires inputs of shape (n_samples, n_features), whereas BTE operates on raw multivariate time-series of shape (n_samples, 1024, 12). Two options are available: flattening each task into a single 12,288-dimensional vector, which sacrifices temporal coherence, or specialized time-series interpolation such as DTW-SMOTE [37], which adds implementation complexity without guaranteed gains. We adopt flattening to keep the protocol uniform across all conditions. Under this protocol, BTE-SMOTE Random Forest reaches AUC = 0.842 ± 0.043 in binary and 0.825 ± 0.030 in multiclass,


17
statistically indistinguishable from BTE-NoAug. Figure 1 shows PCA visualization comparing real (purple) and synthetic (yellow) samples generated by ETB-GAN. The strong overlap between distributions is consistent with the low C2ST AUC (0.458), supporting the validity of the latent-space approach for training generative models.
Figure 1. PCA visualization of the real (purple) and synthetic (yellow) distributions
Privacy (DCR)
The Distance to Closest Record reveals a tradeoff between realism and privacy. ETB-GAN reaches a minimum DCR of 0.000, indicating that the generator may reproduce some real samples; this near-zero distance is computed in the 90dimensional latent-space, where the autoencoder embedding is a non-invertible lossy projection, so it does not by itself imply identifiability at the level of raw patient signals. BTE-GAN reaches a much higher DCR (3.435), but this distance reflects the generation of unrealistic artifacts rather than novel, realistic patient profiles, consistent with its poor fidelity scores. For any future clinical deployment sharing synthetic
cohorts, this trade-off means privacy distance alone cannot certify safety and must be interpreted jointly with the fidelity metrics reported above. Table 7 shows Generative quality of ETB-GAN evaluated in the shared 90dimensional embedding space (mean ± std over 5 seeds). C2ST AUC, Wasserstein distance, and discriminator accuracy indicate better performance when lower; DCR indicates better performance when higher.
