5.3 Statistical Analysis
We assessed augmentation and pipeline effects with paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests on the 25 matched fold-seed AUC measurements, across all 108 condition pairs (Bonferroni-corrected, α = 0.05). Ten pairs reached significance, all involving the weaker classifiers (SVM, MLP, and Logistic Regression); no comparison involving the three strongest classifiers (Random Forest, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost) was flagged. The detected differences correspond to AUC gaps of 0.026 to 0.059: statistically detectable given the


18
low cross-validation variance, but modest in absolute terms. Among the 48 comparisons of an augmentation condition against the unaugmented baseline, only two crossed the threshold, and they pointed in opposite directions (SMOTE improved the MLP and degraded the SVM in ETB multiclass), confirming the absence of a systematic gain from augmentation.
We also examined whether augmentation reinforces demographic disparities, computing classification performance separately for demographic subgroups across the 25 fold-seed splits — a fairness check we consider a necessary component of any clinical AI evaluation, not an optional add-on. Classification AUC was higher for female than for male patients, and this gap was essentially unaffected by augmentation (male-female AUC gap: -0.063 without augmentation versus -0.068 with GAN); we therefore find no evidence that latent-space GAN augmentation reinforces a gender performance gap. For the early-versus-late-onset contrast, GAN augmentation was associated with a reduction in early-onset PD detection recall (from 0.92 to 0.88) and a widening of the onset recall gap (from +0.019 to +0.040); however, the earlyonset subgroup comprises only 67 patients (~13 per test fold) and the per-fold recall standard deviation (~0.10) precludes a firm conclusion. We report this as a preliminary observation that warrants a dedicated, adequately powered investigation before any augmentation strategy affecting early-onset detection is adopted clinically.
