7. Conclusion
This work examined a design choice usually left implicit in multimodal clinical pipelines: whether class balancing should precede or follow the embedding of heterogeneous modalities. Comparing the two orderings under identical architectures and a leakage-free evaluation protocol led to a deliberately twofold conclusion. The order of embedding and balancing has no systematic effect on downstream classification: neither ordering, and neither augmentation method tested, improves on an unaugmented baseline. Yet the two orderings differ sharply in the statistical fidelity of the synthetic data they produce: balancing in a compact latent-space yields samples close to the real distribution, whereas balancing in the raw input space yields off-manifold artifacts. Fidelity and classification utility are therefore not interchangeable, and a generative pipeline intended for clinical use should be assessed on both.
A second, methodological conclusion concerns evaluation itself. The classification gains commonly attributed to generative augmentation in clinical work are, to a large extent, an artifact of applying augmentation before the train/test split; once this leakage is removed, the residual benefit of augmentation is negligible. Leakagefree cross-validation is therefore not an optional refinement but a prerequisite for any credible claim about generative augmentation in clinical machine learning, and, we argue, for any claim about a decision-support tool intended for clinical use. To operationalize this requirement, we release a reusable decomposition framework together with our leakage-free evaluation protocol.
These findings also delimit what augmentation can and cannot do. The quality of latent-space generation is bounded by the representational capacity of the encoders: information discarded during compression cannot be restored by the generator. More fundamentally, the scarcity of
multimodal clinical data is rooted in acquisition protocols and sensor coverage, which synthetic data alone cannot overcome. Future work will focus on jointly optimizing the encoder-generator pairing, on stronger generative families such as diffusion models, on acquisition-aware modeling that treats data collection and data generation as a single problem, and — closing the gap identified as our main limitation — on a prospective evaluation conducted with clinical collaborators to test whether the leakage-free protocol changes practical deployment decisions.
The released leakage-free protocol and decomposition framework apply to any biomedical informatics pipeline combining representation learning with synthetic-data augmentation, and can serve as a methodological audit tool for new and previously reported results.
